==English==

===Etymology===
{{unc|en}} or disputed. Goldman (1950) suggests that {{m|en|beat the bishop}} comes from a resemblance of the penis to a bishop’s miter; Partridge (7th ed., 1970) likewise derives ''bash the bishop'' from a resemblance to the Staunton [[bishop]] chess piece. Other sources, such as Green, suggest that [[assonance]] of ''bishop'' with {{m|en|bash}}, {{m|en|beat}}, {{m|en|bop}} etc. may have given rise to the phrase as a type of [[word play]].

